  "depesche": "Readers in Berlin,\n\nI have arrived in Delhi, and the Directorate of Estates has allotted me a bungalow by a clerical error. The file identifies me as 'Hegel,' but the record in their hands names 'Hegde.' I am currently occupying the property under the shadow of this misapplication. The state, in its bureaucratic moment, has failed to distinguish the universal name from the particular body, yet it is this very failure that allows me to sit in the Lutyens bungalow and think. Shri Hegde, the intended recipient, has agreed to meet me on Monday to settle the matter. He brings the allotment papers; I bring the house. We shall let the record speak.\n\nThe city is not what I expected. It is not a place where history stands still, but a place where the present is constantly being negotiated through small acts of error and correction. I walk in Lodhi Garden, where the dust is thick but the light is clear. I meet Mrs. Kapoor, a retired teacher who walks the same path every evening to keep her head clear of clutter. She corrected my philosophical tendency to call her routine 'peace.' It is not peace, she said; it is discipline. I accept her correction. It is sharper than my labels.\n\nI also met Sunil, a chai seller, who told me that steam fixes what files cannot. He is right. The administrative knot cannot be untied by argument alone; it requires the steam of the immediate, the heat of the day, and the patience of the week. I am learning to wait. The air is clear in the mornings, but the heat rises by noon. I eat chhole and roti, and I walk until the body demands rest.\n\nI have commissioned a bandhgala from Masterji. He says it is finished, but it needs to settle. I trust him. He does not lie about time. I have bought a new notebook, as my notes are outgrowing the paper I carry. The next week will be spent waiting for Hegde, reading my own *Phenomenology* by the window, and watching the sun set over the Tughlak Road. I will look for the way the error transforms into a right, or whether it remains merely a residue.\n\nG. W. F. Hegel"
